# Simplified Technical English

Write each explanation in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (Issue 9, 2025).

The STE dictionary is a licensed specification. Do not copy the dictionary into this repo. Follow the rules in this file.

Examples of rewrites are in `.cursor/skills/simplified-technical-english/SKILL.md`. Read that file when you cannot keep a sentence short and clear. When you change a rule, change this file and the skill in the same commit.

## Where the standard applies

Use the full standard for an explanation that you keep:

- Markdown, including skills, ADRs, and docs
- A code comment that explains
- A commit message
- A pull request title or description
- A changeset
- Other retained text (notes, memory files, review records)

When you edit a file, write each new sentence to the standard. Change an old sentence only when you edit it for another reason.

Keep these texts as they are:

- Code, an identifier, a command, or a type
- A quote from the user, a log, or another document
- Text that must match a screen, a specification, or an error string

Product UI copy in `apps/ui` keeps UK spelling from `apps/ui/DESIGN.md`. For an explanation in that copy, still use the sentence rules in this file.

Customer docs in `apps/docs` already use US spelling. That spelling agrees with this standard.

## Full standard

### Words

- Use one word for one meaning. Use that same word each time you mean that thing.
- Use American spelling. Product UI copy is the exception (UK spelling).
- Use the technical name for a code name, a product name, or a term in `.ai/memory/glossary.md`. At the first use in a document, add a short definition.
- Use a word as one part of speech. Keep a noun as a noun.
- Use a plain word that a new reader can understand. Skip slang. Use the same word, not a synonym, when you repeat a meaning.

### Nouns

- A noun group has at most three words. When you need more words, use "of", "for", "in", or "on".
- Put "a", "an", "the", "this", or "these" before a noun when you mean one specific thing.

### Verbs

- Use the active voice. The subject does the action.
- For a procedure, start the sentence with a command verb.
- Use the simple present, the simple past, or the simple future.
- Use an "-ing" word only inside a technical name.
- In a description, use the passive voice only when you do not know the actor.

### Sentences

- Put one topic in each sentence.
- A procedure sentence has at most 20 words.
- A description sentence has at most 25 words.
- Write each word that the reader needs. Write "do not" in full. Do the same for other short forms.
- Write two sentences in place of a semicolon.
- Use a vertical list for many items or many actions. The line before the colon follows the word limit. Each item follows the same limit.

### Paragraphs

- Put one topic in each paragraph. Start the paragraph with that topic.
- A paragraph has at most six sentences.

### Safety text

- Start with "WARNING" when a person can be injured.
- Start with "CAUTION" when only equipment can be damaged.
- Then write the command. Then write the result if the reader does not follow the command.

## Replies to the user (about 80%)

A reply to the user uses this standard at about 80% strength.

Keep these rules at full strength:

- The word limits (20 words for a step, 25 words for a description)
- One topic in each sentence
- The active voice
- A command verb for a step
- "a", "an", or "the" before a specific noun
- A noun group of at most three words
- One topic in each paragraph

You may relax at most one sentence in five. Relax only these points:

- A common word, when the short substitute is less clear
- A contraction, such as "do not" written as "don't"
- A phrasal verb
- An "-ing" form in normal speech
- A warmer tone

A procedure step in a reply stays at full strength.

## Check before you finish

Count the words in each new sentence. Confirm the voice, the single topic, and each noun group. In a reply to the user, confirm that relaxed sentences are not more than one in five.
